      "settings": {
        "eyebrow": "Virtual staging near you",
        "heading": "Virtual Staging NYC",
        "lede": "Virtual staging is a digital marketing technique that adds furniture and decor to real estate photographs: an interior designer furnishes empty apartments and cleared rooms in the image so buyers can see scale and layout, with nothing carried up the service elevator. At Manhattan and Brooklyn prices every listing photo carries real value — Bella's designers stage NYC listings in 24 to 48 hours, from {price:virtual-staging} a photo.",
        "cta_label": "Stage my listing — from {price:virtual-staging}",
        "cta_link": "/products/virtual-staging",
        "alt_label": "Browse the lookbook",
        "alt_link": "/pages/lookbook",
        "city": "New York City",
        "market_heading": "Why virtual staging matters in the NYC market",
        "market_text": "<p>New York buyers make snap judgments on StreetEasy, and the city's housing stock makes empty photos brutal: pre-war co-ops with narrow rooms, new-development glass boxes that all look alike, and railroad layouts buyers can't mentally furnish. Home staging proves a queen bed fits in the second bedroom — the question that decides whether an NYC showing gets booked.</p><p>Physical staging in Manhattan and Brooklyn is a serious expense: furniture rental, a service elevator booking, a certificate of insurance for the building, and a crew who can work within board rules. Virtual staging skips all of it, which is why the cost comparison here is more lopsided than in any other US market.</p><p>Co-op and condo board logistics are the hidden cost people forget. A physical stage can stall for weeks waiting on building approval and elevator access; the photos can't wait that long when a listing's first week on StreetEasy determines most of its traffic.</p><p>97% of buyers use the internet in their home search (NAR), and New York buyers compare listing photos on StreetEasy long before a showing. Empty apartments photograph smaller and colder than they are, and staged photos tend to draw more clicks, views and showing requests. Virtual staging answers the scale question with furniture sized to the real room, so buyers can visualize how each space is used. Brokerages that need virtual staging New York buyers believe — including Brown Harris Stevens, a Bella client — use it on everything from pre-war co-ops to new developments.</p>",
        "local_heading": "Virtual staging NYC buyers can trust at the showing",
        "local_text": "<p>Designers stage pre-war co-ops with furniture that respects the mouldings and proportions, new developments with the clean contemporary look those buyers expect, and Brooklyn brownstones somewhere between the two.</p><p>Small-room proof is the priority throughout. In a market where the deciding question is usually whether real furniture fits, staging that quietly oversizes a room does more harm than good — we furnish to scale so the photo survives the showing.</p><p>For the shoot, send wide, high-resolution photos in the best light you can get: daylight, lamps on, blinds open. Our designers match the lighting, textures and ceiling height in each photo so the furnishings sit realistically against the walls, and every final image is ready for the REBNY RLS and StreetEasy with disclosure guidance. Developers and teams marketing several apartments get volume discounts automatically — 5% at 3+ photos, 10% at 6+, 15% at 11+ and 20% at 21+.</p>",
        "hoods_label": "Where we stage most",
        "hoods": "Upper East Side, Upper West Side, Chelsea, Tribeca, Williamsburg, Park Slope, Brooklyn Heights, Long Island City, Harlem, Astoria"
      }
    },
